<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Plan 53 B6 (QBX-164): A LANE THE RECORDER COULD NOT PLACE ON IS SAID OUT
  LOUD, and the pass still lands everywhere else.

  Until now a skipped lane was a TW_LOGW and a `continue`. From the user's
  side that is indistinguishable from the take never having been recorded:
  they armed the lane, watched the meters move, pressed stop, and got nothing,
  with the only explanation in a log nobody reads. The capture file is still on
  disk in every case, so the two things worth reporting are which lane missed
  out and that its audio was kept.

  WHY THIS CASE COULD NOT BE WRITTEN BEFORE. The refusal it needs did not
  exist: `LegacyTakes` hands a wrapped take column to `add-take` like any
  other, so nothing refused a lane at all. QBX-172 gave `takes-split` and
  `replace` the G11 rule - a wrapped column refuses the whole lane for the pass
  - which is why plan 53 made B6 wait for P1.

  THE FIXTURE. takestack_wrap_wrapperpan.qxp holds the WRAPPED shape (Kw):
  track 0's placement is an SCut over an STakeStack of two takes, covering
  [0, 96000). Track 1 is empty. The wrapped shape is the one every planner
  refuses, and it is a real project file rather than something assembled here,
  because no verb builds that shape on purpose any more.

  TWO ARMED LANES, ONE REFUSAL, which is the whole design of the case:

    track 0   record mode `replace`   holds a WRAPPED column   -> REFUSED
    track 1   record mode `takes`     empty                    -> takes the pass

  so it asserts the two things that matter together. The refusal is REPORTED,
  and it costs the OTHER lane nothing - a band punch where one lane refuses
  must not throw away the lanes that were fine. That second half is why this
  is not simply a one-track case: `commitPlacement_` places per lane inside one
  macro, and an early return or a thrown-away composite would take the good
  lane with it.

  ONE REPORT PER PASS, NOT ONE PER LANE. `skipped=1` is read off the
  recorder's own `describe()` through `assert-recorded-clip contains=`, which
  the audio verb did not have until this change - its MIDI sibling has had it
  since proposal 21 L4. That asymmetry is why the recorder's report would
  otherwise have been unassertable, and it is fixed here.

  `unused=1` is the other half: the capture file that no lane took is listed
  (plan 53 Q13). The LIST is this ticket's; the Unused recordings dock that
  presents it is P6's.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED. The status-bar line
  `SMainWindow::onRecordingLanesSkipped` writes. No verb reads the transient
  status message, and adding one for a single string would be a worse trade
  than saying plainly that the surface is hand-verified while the CONTENT it
  shows - the count and the reason - is gated here and in the two assert-log
  lines below. The PR says the same.

  Env, set on the CTest entry: SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_BACKEND=file:../test_sawtooth.wav
  (STEREO - test_position.wav is mono and cannot host channel 2),
  SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_LATENCY_FRAMES=4800, SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED=1; RUN_SERIAL.

  ON main THIS FAILS at the first assert-log: the lane is skipped silently,
  `describe()` has no `skipped=` field at all, and `assert-recorded-clip` has
  no `contains=` to read it with.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="record_lane_skipped_reported">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <load-project path="../takestack_wrap_wrapperpan.qxp"/>

    <!-- The fixture's shape, asserted before anything is recorded: a WRAPPED
         column on track 0, which is what gets refused. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="wrapped" takes="2"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="96000"/>

    <!-- Track 0 refuses: `replace` will not touch a wrapped column. -->
    <set-track-record-mode trackPath="0" mode="replace"/>
    <assert-record-mode trackPath="0" resolved="replace"/>
    <!-- Track 1 is left at the project default, which is `takes`. -->
    <assert-record-mode trackPath="1" resolved="takes"/>

    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="0" mode="off"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="1" mode="off"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="audio:qxa-record-input:1"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="1" input="audio:qxa-record-input:2"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="1" armed="1"/>

    <!-- The pass starts at 0 and runs about a second, so it overlaps track 0's
         [0, 96000) column however the wall clock jitters - two seconds of
         material against a pass of one leaves thousands of frames of room on
         both sides, which is why no exact landing is asserted anywhere here. -->
    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <toggle-playback play="1"/>
    <wait-playhead minAdvance="4096" timeoutMs="30000"/>
    <wait-ms ms="500"/>
    <record-start/>
    <wait-ms ms="1200"/>
    <!-- nestedRejects="1": the ONE refusal inside this action's window is the
         planner refusing track 0, which is the point of the case. The runner
         decides whether an action failed by sampling the history's rejection
         counter around its submit and cannot tell this action's own outcome
         from that of anything it submitted while applying — so without this
         the deliberate refusal would read as `record-stop` being broken. It is
         an EXACT count, so record-stop genuinely failing would make it 2 and
         still fail the case. -->
    <record-stop nestedRejects="1"/>

    <!-- THE REFUSAL IS ANNOUNCED, by the planner and then by the recorder. -->
    <assert-log contains="[REC] lane refused the pass" minCount="1"/>
    <assert-log contains="did not take the recorded pass" minCount="1"/>

    <!-- THE OTHER LANE STILL GOT ITS TAKE, and the recorder reports exactly
         one skipped lane and one unused capture file. `contains=` reads
         SAudioRecorder::describe(), which is global to the pass, so asserting
         it on this lane's call is deliberate and not a mix-up. -->
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="1" clips="1" passes="1"
                          minDurationFrames="24000"
                          contains="skipped=1 unused=1"/>

    <!-- TRACK 0 IS EXACTLY AS IT WAS: the wrapped column untouched, and no
         second child - the pass was not placed over it either. -->
    <assert-take-column clip="0,0" shape="wrapped" takes="2"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0" duration="96000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" expectReject="true"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
